  "query_preview": "..\nNext 7 days (through Wednesday)...\n\nSatellite imagery showed a large upper low over the Pacific NW \ncoast and an upper ridge over the central CONUS. This placed a \nstrong southwest flow across our region. The pattern presents fire\nweather concerns for much of south central Montana. Recent \nobservations show southwest winds gusting into the 30-40 mph range\nin and near the Beartooth Foothills with winds expected to mix \nout to the 20-30 mph range from around Yellowstone County \nwestward late today. In the meantime, humidity levels have \ndropped to around 20% or less in these areas. A Red Flag Warning \ncontinues for these areas until 900 PM. \n\nSouthwest winds will remain breezy out of the southwest for Friday\nas well. Downslope flow and drier air will drop afternoon humidity \ndown into the teens and possibly even single digit readings over \nthe eastern plains. Temps soar into the upper 80s and 90s. Winds \nwill be borderline of criteria for many locations, but are \nexpected to be strongest over the Big Horns region into southeast \nMontana (20-35 mph gusts). Thus, we have upgraded a fire weather \nwatch to a Red Flag Warning for our southeast zones through 900 \npm Friday. \n\nEnsembles agree the upper low to the west will shift eastward into\nthe inter-mountain Rockies late this weekend. This will lead to a\ngradual cooling trend into next week and an increasing chance of \nshowers and some thunderstorms as energy rounds the bottom of the \nupper trough and tracks across our area with a strong diffluent \nflow developing. Look for scattered shower and thunderstorm \nactivity across most of the CWA by Sunday night into Monday. Heavy\nrain is possible as PWATs jump to over an inch for the eastern \ntwo thirds of our CWA. Temps by Monday will range from lower 70s\nwest to mid 80s east. \n\nModels suggest the pattern will turn somewhat benign into the \nmiddle of next week, with temperatures around to slightly below \nnormal (70s-low 80s) and a slight chance (10-20%) of daily \nshowers and thunderstorms, mainly over the high country. BT",
